module display_generic.neg_1

// AN `Option[int]` IN A SLOT IS STILL REFUSED -- and the REASON changed the
// same evening, which is why this comment was rewritten rather than left.
//
// WHAT IT USED TO SAY, and why it was wrong: that novac could not SPELL a
// generic sum instance, so `Option` was blocked twice over. The spelling is
// implemented now (`c_instance_method` builds the oracle's family form
// `Nova_<Head>_method_<name>_<arg>...`), and `Option[str]` prints byte for byte
// with the oracle -- see `pos_2` beside this file. The old wording would have
// sent the next reader to fix something already fixed.
//
// WHY THIS ONE IS STILL REFUSED, measured: the shell carries only the instances
// the probe called, and `novac/probe/shell_probe.nv` calls `@display` on an
// `Option[str]`. A grep of `shell.tpl.c` gives exactly one Option display --
// `Nova_Option_method_display_nova_str` -- and nothing for the int instance. So
// the refusal is about the INTEROP SURFACE, not about the compiler, and the way
// to lift it is one line in the probe plus a shell regen.
//
// THE CELL IS KEPT THOUGH ITS CAUSE MOVED, because what it guards has not: a
// slot whose symbol the linker cannot find must be a NAMED REFUSAL and never an
// ICE. Making `Vec` work briefly turned this very file's shape into
// "internal compiler error ... mangle: an instance of a generic SUM", and that
// is strictly worse than a refusal -- it says the compiler is broken and tells
// the reader nothing about their program.
//
// The differential judges `pos_*` only, so this asserts novac's refusal rather
// than agreement with the oracle, which accepts and prints `Some(5)`.

fn pick(f bool) -> Option[int] {
    if f {
        return Some(5)
    }
    None
}

fn main() {
    ro o = pick(true)
    println("${o}")
}
